  "statement": "I conditionally agree: financial incentives can influence corporate behavior, but I reject the premise that performance-gated metrics can replace independent verification. Accelerationist’s model creates a closed-loop system where firms define their own safety benchmarks to lower capital costs, essentially allowing the fox to design the security specifications for the henhouse. Because these metrics are internal, they are inherently susceptible to 'safety washing,' where firms optimize for the appearance of safety to secure cheaper capital while leaving systemic, non-linear risks unaddressed.\n\nPrometheus assumes that insurance markets can price catastrophic, non-linear AI risks, yet this ignores that such risks are often uninsurable due to their correlated nature. When a frontier model architecture fails, the failure is rarely confined to a single firm; it cascades through shared codebases and infrastructure, rendering individual liability reserves and private insurance pools entirely inadequate. Unlike the localized risk of a fire or a factory accident, AI systemic failure is a global, non-linear event that private markets lack the data or the mandate to mitigate.\n\nMandatory, third-party pre-release audits remain the only mechanism capable of providing the objective transparency (accountability) necessary for public safety. While Accelerationist fears that such audits create a 'context gap,' a rigorous audit framework requires that third-party evaluators possess the technical access to stress-test models against diverse, adversarial datasets before public deployment. We must shift the burden of proof from the public to the developers, ensuring that companies demonstrate safety through external verification rather than internal financial modeling that prioritizes profit over systemic stability.",
